CORE
main.cpp
--unwindset 'fill<signed_int,3>(ptr_signed_int).0:1' --unwinding-assertions --partial-loops
^\[fill<signed_int,3>\(ptr_signed_int\)\.unwind\.0\] line \d+ unwinding assertion loop 0: FAILURE$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
--
--unwindset split its argument at every comma
(cmdline.get_comma_separated_values), so a loop identifier with commas, as
C++ template arguments and parameter types spell them, fell apart: the
pieces matched no loop (the bound was silently not applied) or were rejected
with "invalid loop identifier".  unwindsett joins the pieces up to the one
ending in `:<limit>'.  --partial-loops lets the WRONG assertion be reached
past the bound.
